          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Leaven primarily symbolizes sin, corrupt conduct, hypocrisy, or false teaching whose influence spreads through a community, while some contexts use it positively for the kingdom’s pervasive growth.\",\"book_summary\":\"Leaven symbolizes corrupt teaching or conduct that operates unnoticed and eventually permeates the whole body.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"A corrupting moral or doctrinal influence that spreads through the whole.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Corrupt teaching or conduct that spreads unseen through the whole body.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The consensus recognizes positive kingdom uses of leaven in Matthew 13:33 and Luke 13:20–21, whereas the entry states only the predominant negative sense. Because the entry does not claim that leaven is invariably negative, this omission does not establish a conflicting boundary.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book directly matches the consensus’s primary negative identification and its defining feature of pervasive spread.\",\"rationale\":\"After reducing both readings to their minimum core, each identifies leaven with spreading doctrinal or moral corruption. The book’s categories of corrupt teaching and conduct correspond to the consensus’s false teaching, hypocrisy, sin, and corruption. Its language about working unseen and permeating the whole makes the mechanism more vivid but does not materially alter the referent or covered cases.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The entry omits the widely recognized positive kingdom usage, and its cited verses do not all explicitly articulate the hidden spreading process. Still, Matthew 16:12 directly supports corrupt teaching, while 1 Corinthians 5:7–8 directly supports corrupt conduct and the leaven metaphor, so this does not undermine the core match.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
